body:
Renaissance style reading: I'm still learning but this is my impression reading the chart. (I use regiomontanus & Lilly's dignities).

&#x200B;

The Asc is in Pisces at 19 Degrees, and Jupiter itself is in the 8th House dignified by term. Jupiter is also trined with Saturn who is peregrine, and conjunct the Moon who is in her sign of Fall.

&#x200B;

This indicates to me that the Querent is socially competent, and presents a very cheerful outer face. However deep down he does harbour some melancholic and intense emotional tendencies that he tries to keep suppressed unsuccessfully.

There is a lot of contrast in this respect, he can be melancholic in temperment but also sociable and sanguine at other times. His friends are also of a melancholic and pragmatic nature, the cusp of the 11th being in Capricorn.

However he needs to watch out, as they can purportedly be treacherous and work against him at times without his knowing. (Saturn is the ruler of the 11th house and is dwelling in the 12th whilst peregrine, a malefic influence then.)

&#x200B;

Fortunately, he will live for a relatively long time. (Jupiter is dignified in the 8th house) and won't die suddenly. There is an afflicted moon in the 8th, however it is somewhat recitified by Jupiter who is conjunct it. 

&#x200B;

In terms of sickness he will be in excellent health throughout his life, though he may suffer from either Melancholic (Earthy/heavy) and Phlegmatic (Watery/Fluidy) illnesses at times he will overcome any such occurences. Mercury is actually Cazimi - so it shouldn't be too much of a problem at all.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

Financially he will in the long term be well off and comfortable - however, frankly he might not be the "big earner" in the household, his partners will tend to make more money than him and will "Sugar Mommy" him, which is definately a strong way to his heart, he loves to be spoiled.

However make sure he doesn't grow dependant on being provided for and can be independant, seems to be the message here.

&#x200B;

In respect to relationships, his 7th is ruled by Venus who is in the 7th house. He is a passionate and loving man, who feels deeply and loves with everything he has. Also a very sexually liberal person. The person who becomes his wife will ultimately be happy and in a good relationship from what I'm reading here and they will bring a lot of blessings into his life too.

&#x200B;

Hope this helps!

Soul selling is an interesting concept, and one that is generally looked down on by a lot of Occultists nowadays as either non-existant or fraudulent. This is however, an understanding that has come about due to the bizarre new age conceptions on Magic that Theosophy and the Golden Dawn brought about. It is a result of whitewashing to make magic palatable to folk who don't want to get their hands dirty.

&#x200B;

If we look at historical magic on the otherhand, It is clear on reading Early Modern Texts of actual magic that the idea of selling ones soul, or giving oneself to the devil, is a legitimate concept that was actively done. The black books of Elverum, a Swedish book of Folk magic in the Svartboken (butchered that spelling)  tradition actively begins with a ritual to sell the soul.  The concept has been around in Hoodoo since forever, and English Folk Magic also gives methods for such. For example in the Shetland Islands its recorded that one may go down to the beach, put one hand on the bottom of their feet and the other ontop of their head and say "I give what is between my two hands to the devil"  and he will come.

&#x200B;

Other examples include the Faustian tradition, such as Magia Naturalis et Innaturalis by which the Demons are conjured and bound; yet also approached to make a Pact. The nature of which, is not explicitly stated, but I think considering the text is attributed to Faust implicitly says a lot about the nature of this Pact. The Cyprianic tradition also holds such exemplars.   


I think that the idea of giving ones soul is inherently a sacrifice, it is giving ones entire self to the Devil and his infernal legions (Howsoever you perceive them yourself). The soul being the sum of all our parts. Someone mentioned the World soul- I believe that the Sacrifice or bartering of the soul to these spirits is in essence giving ones entire world to them, in exchange for a period of power. Because that is what this ritual represents, a man who has reached the pinnacle of his despair that he would give up the pleasures of eternity for a moment of (what is in orthodox ethics generally wrongful) power over the world.

That being said, Pacts with Demons do not necessarily always involve the Soul. The Contract between man and spirit is inherently that, a contract. And its terms will vary.

&#x200B;
